WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.850
Each night, one program pays every shop what it took that day.

2
00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:12.272
Three copies of it run on three machines, so one can die: job A, job B and job C.

3
00:00:12.272 --> 00:00:14.816
Only one may pay the shops each night.

4
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.809
One night, job A freezes for 15 seconds.

5
00:00:17.809 --> 00:00:20.337
Job B takes over and pays the shops.

6
00:00:20.337 --> 00:00:23.216
Then job A wakes up, and pays them again.

7
00:00:23.216 --> 00:00:25.603
How do we stop that second payout?

8
00:00:25.603 --> 00:00:26.937
Hold that question.

9
00:00:27.167 --> 00:00:30.561
Picture the ticket machine at a busy post office.

10
00:00:30.561 --> 00:00:31.877
You pull ticket 47.

11
00:00:31.877 --> 00:00:35.548
When it lights up, it's your turn, and nobody else's.

12
00:00:35.548 --> 00:00:38.457
Wander off, and the screen moves on to 48.

13
00:00:38.667 --> 00:00:46.307
Our ticket machine is etcd, a small service that a few servers run together, agreeing on every change.

14
00:00:46.307 --> 00:00:55.671
Each copy creates a key under one shared name, settlement, and etcd stamps it with a number from a counter that only goes up.

15
00:00:55.671 --> 00:00:57.993
Job A got 7, job B 9, job C 11.

16
00:00:58.167 --> 00:01:00.805
The lowest ticket leads, so job A leads.

17
00:01:00.805 --> 00:01:03.840
Each key is tied to a lease, ten seconds here.

18
00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.743
A copy keeps its lease alive by checking in.

19
00:01:06.743 --> 00:01:10.767
Stop checking in, the lease runs out, and the key is deleted.

20
00:01:10.767 --> 00:01:12.152
That's wandering off.

21
00:01:12.333 --> 00:01:15.925
Job B watches job A's key, and job C watches B's.

22
00:01:15.925 --> 00:01:19.664
When a leader goes, only the next in line wakes up.

23
00:01:19.933 --> 00:01:27.632
Inside one program, a lock means one holder at a time, and etcd's election sounds like the same promise.

24
00:01:27.632 --> 00:01:30.075
So job A checks: am I the leader?

25
00:01:30.075 --> 00:01:30.371
Yes.

26
00:01:30.371 --> 00:01:36.664
And it pays batch 17 into the payout ledger, the table where each payout is recorded.

27
00:01:36.833 --> 00:01:39.894
Now job A's program stops for 15 seconds.

28
00:01:39.894 --> 00:01:44.372
Java pauses everything to clean up memory, or a disk stalls.

29
00:01:44.372 --> 00:01:46.387
Its check-ins stop with it.

30
00:01:46.387 --> 00:01:51.089
To etcd, a frozen program and a dead one look exactly the same.

31
00:01:51.267 --> 00:01:56.755
The lease runs out, and etcd deletes job A's key, as change number 12.

32
00:01:56.755 --> 00:01:58.323
Job B's watch fires.

33
00:01:58.323 --> 00:02:03.105
Its key is now the lowest, so job B leads, and pays batch 18.

34
00:02:03.267 --> 00:02:07.471
Then job A wakes, and finishes the payout it was writing when it froze.

35
00:02:07.471 --> 00:02:12.091
It checked it was leader before the freeze, and nothing has told it otherwise.

36
00:02:12.233 --> 00:02:16.144
Well, the shops in batch 17 may just have been paid twice.

37
00:02:16.433 --> 00:02:17.977
And this isn't rare.

38
00:02:17.977 --> 00:02:29.396
In 2020, Jepsen, a project that tests databases by breaking them, saw two holders of an etcd lock overlap within minutes, even in a healthy cluster.

39
00:02:29.667 --> 00:02:32.962
Couldn't job A just check again, right before it writes?

40
00:02:32.962 --> 00:02:39.259
It should, but the freeze can land between the check and the write, and from inside, a freeze is invisible.

41
00:02:39.467 --> 00:02:44.423
What we want is someone who sees the write at the moment it arrives, and can say no.

42
00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:46.219
Back to the post office.

43
00:02:46.219 --> 00:02:49.384
The clerk who has called 48 won't serve ticket 47.

44
00:02:49.384 --> 00:02:56.283
So let every write carry the writer's ticket number, and let the ledger remember the highest it has accepted.

45
00:02:56.467 --> 00:02:57.254
Replay it.

46
00:02:57.254 --> 00:02:59.851
Job A pays batch 17 with token 7.

47
00:02:59.851 --> 00:03:03.550
Nothing higher has been seen, so it's accepted.

48
00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:06.147
Job B pays batch 18 with token 9.

49
00:03:06.147 --> 00:03:11.263
9 is higher than 7, so it's accepted, and the ledger remembers 9.

50
00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.397
Now job A wakes and writes with token 7.

51
00:03:14.397 --> 00:03:16.195
What does the ledger do?

52
00:03:16.195 --> 00:03:17.393
Pause and guess.

53
00:03:20.633 --> 00:03:21.510
It refuses.

54
00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:22.946
7 is lower than 9.

55
00:03:22.946 --> 00:03:27.331
One copy paid, one copy was turned away at the counter.

56
00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:31.248
That's called fencing, and the number is a fencing token.

57
00:03:31.248 --> 00:03:32.463
Here it comes free.

58
00:03:32.463 --> 00:03:39.951
The key's revision was handed out by etcd's agreement, so no two leaders share one, and every new leader's is higher.

59
00:03:40.167 --> 00:03:42.146
Job A's next check-in fails.

60
00:03:42.146 --> 00:03:47.730
Now it knows it lost, so it rejoins at the back, with a new key at revision 13.

61
00:03:47.730 --> 00:03:49.851
A token never comes back down.

62
00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:51.640
One rule keeps it honest.

63
00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:56.690
The check must happen at the ledger, in the same all or nothing step as the write.

64
00:03:56.690 --> 00:04:00.940
Check in one step and write in the next, and you've reopened the gap.

65
00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:04.143
In a SQL database, that's one conditional statement.

66
00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:06.477
And the lease length is a trade.

67
00:04:06.477 --> 00:04:13.076
After a crash in the lesson's run, the next job waited 56 seconds, the rest of a 60-second lease.

68
00:04:13.076 --> 00:04:16.885
Shorter leases take over faster, with more false alarms.

69
00:04:17.133 --> 00:04:20.384
So the moral of the story: a lease says who should act.

70
00:04:20.384 --> 00:04:23.397
Only the resource can refuse the one who shouldn't.

71
00:04:23.667 --> 00:04:24.959
One quick check.

72
00:04:24.959 --> 00:04:29.404
Job B freezes next, and job C takes over with token 11.

73
00:04:29.404 --> 00:04:32.716
Then job B wakes and writes with token 9.

74
00:04:32.716 --> 00:04:34.333
Accepted or refused?

75
00:04:34.333 --> 00:04:35.625
Pause and think.

76
00:04:38.867 --> 00:04:39.554
Refused.

77
00:04:39.554 --> 00:04:43.765
Once the ledger has seen 11, every 9 is old news.
